// Ordering comparisons: one rule, no second-class digit script
//
// `<`, `<=`, `>`, `>=` are:
//   · numeric       when both sides are numbers — a string counts as a number
//                   when `#|…|` would convert it, in any of the 69 scripts
//   · lexicographic when both sides are non-numeric text
//   · an error      when a number meets text that is not a number
//
// So `"१०" > "९"` asks what `"10" > "9"` asks: 10 against 9, not the order of
// their code points. `"५" > 5` coerces exactly as `"5" > 5` does. Anything else
// would make every script but ASCII second-class in its own language.
//
// Equality is deliberately outside the rule: `==` never coerces, so `"5" == 5`
// and `"५" == 5` are both false — again, the same answer for both scripts.

>> "--- numeric strings, ASCII digits ---" ¶
>> ("10" > "9") ¶
>> ("9" > "10") ¶
>> ("5" > 5) ¶
>> ("6" > 5) ¶
>> (5 > "4") ¶
>> ("1.5" > "1.4") ¶

>> "--- numeric strings, Devanagari digits: same answers ---" ¶
>> ("१०" > "९") ¶
>> ("९" > "१०") ¶
>> ("५" > 5) ¶
>> ("६" > 5) ¶
>> (5 > "४") ¶
>> ("१.५" > "१.४") ¶

>> "--- mixed scripts still compare as numbers ---" ¶
>> ("४२" > "9") ¶
>> ("42" > "९") ¶

>> "--- <= and >= follow the same rule ---" ¶
>> ("१०" >= "१०") ¶
>> ("९" <= "१०") ¶

>> "--- non-numeric text stays lexicographic ---" ¶
>> ("abc" > "abd") ¶
>> ("abd" > "abc") ¶

>> "--- chars order by code point, bools #0 < #1 ---" ¶
>> ('b' > 'a') ¶
>> (#1 > #0) ¶

>> "--- equality never coerces ---" ¶
>> ("5" == 5) ¶
>> ("५" == 5) ¶
>> ("5" == "5") ¶

#०९#

>> "--- and the results themselves follow the active mode ---" ¶
>> ("१०" > "९") ¶
>> ("abc" > "abd") ¶

#09#
